 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Heat drives off water and carbon dioxide and leaves sodium carbonate — the main salt in kansui.</li><li>More alkalinity makes the flour hold more water and gives the springy, chewy bite that stays firm in hot broth, plus the faint yellow colour and that typical ramen smell.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why bake baking soda?"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>These are starting points from the Momofuku / Lucky Peach recipe (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"400\" data-s=\"0\">400 g</span> flour, <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"12\" data-s=\"0\">12 g</span> baked soda, <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"200\" data-s=\"0\">200 ml</span> water).</li><li>The dough is stiff on purpose; if it really won’t come together after the first rest, add water a teaspoon at a time.</li><li>The magazine version used all-purpose flour only — the bread flour here adds chew.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Amounts"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Adapted from the Momofuku (David Chang) / Lucky Peach method (Lucky Peach issue 1, 2011), using Harold McGee’s baked baking soda.</li><li>Written in my own words.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Based on"
  }
 ],
